  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nClusters depict strong agreement in the synoptic evolution \nthrough midweek before uncertainty increases with the placement \nand magnitude of the ridge centered over New Mexico. By Thursday\nridge rider activity could resume though most machine learning \nguidance keeps activity more along the high plains rather than \nthis far east, something to monitor. At the same time we above \nnormal temperatures continue through much of the workweek with \nwidespread upper 80s to low 90s into next weekend, several days \nwill feature highs in the mid to upper 90s primarily in the Red \nRiver Valley. Along with the hot temps will be low RH (30%) that\ncould lead near critical fire weather at times through the \nweek. \n\n- Severe risk Saturday/Sunday\n\nA marginal risk is in place across far northwest/north central \nMinnesota this afternoon with a slight risk across all of \nnorthwest Minnesota Sunday. For todays threat the risk is quite \nconditional with a boundary draped NW to SE across North Dakota.\nThis will continue to move east across the state this \nafternoon/evening will the storm risk on the northern periphery.\nA stout post sunset cap will favor any storms to be elevated \nwith hail as the primary threat. Should storms root atop the \ninversion hail to the size of golf balls could be expected but \nmore likely perhaps is a few virga showers as skinny cape \nprofiles will be subject to dry air entrainment limiting and \nupdrafts potential to grow through the hail growth zone. \n\nSimilarly pessimistic about Sunday as the forcing will be weak \nand convective temps anywhere from 95-105. Hitting anything \nabove 100 is far from a guarantee so best chances for storms \nwill increase will eastward extend further away from the 850 \nthermal ridge. Should convective temps be reach surface based \nstorms become likely with initially discrete airmass convection \norganizing into bowing clusters that track southeast. Winds up \nto 60 mph and hail to the size of golf balls are possible.\n\n- Heat Impacts\n\nOur run of 90 degree days in July continues to end the month \nwith 90s possible if not likely each day through next Saturday \nwithin the Red River Valley. Heat headlines have been posted \nthrough the next 2 days for much of the area with a an extreme \nheat warning in effect tomorrow for the central and southern \nvalley as heat indices may locally climb as high as 110. Through\nthe rest of the week the well mix boundary layer and warm temps\nwill routinely lead to highs in the upper 80s to low 90s and \nMin RH around 30%. This will warrant at least marginal fire \nweather concerns most days given the state of seasonally dry to \nvery fuels. Combine this with the potential for one or two days \nto see afternoon winds reaching 20+ mph and near critical \nconditions are possible. Rain looks to be a low probability once\nagain through the coming week possibly further \ndegrading/expanding the existing drought conditions across much \nof the area.",
